dict(pas="2.1", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^為=wéi/conducting, carrying on | 政/government | 以/by means of | 德，/virtue | ^譬/compare it, to liken | 如/to, like | 北辰，/the North Star — the pole of the sky//north//the celestial pole | ^居/(it) dwells, stays | 其/its | 所/place | 而/and | 衆/the many | 星/stars | 共=gǒng/turn toward, face (= 拱 gǒng) | 之。」/it",
  l="The Master said: “Conducting government by virtue —\ncompare it to the North Star:\nit dwells in its place, and the many stars face toward it.”",
  i="The Master said: “Govern by virtue, and you are like the North Star, which keeps still in its place while all the other stars face toward it.”",
  n="The book is named from its first two characters, 為政 'conducting government'. Zhu Xi plays on two old sound-glosses: 政 zhèng 'government' is 正 zhèng 'to set right', and 德 dé 'virtue' is 得 dé 'what one has gained' — the Way practised until it is one's own. 北辰 is the pole of the sky, the point the heavens turn round; Zhu Xi calls it 'the pivot of heaven'. 共 is read gǒng here, the word later written 拱, 'to turn toward, to face as in salute' (Zhu Xi: 共音拱…向也); the everyday reading gòng 'together' does not fit. The picture is of rule without effort: the ruler does not move, and everything orders itself around him."),
# ---------------- 2.2 ----------------
dict(pas="2.2", w="子/the Master | 曰：「《/said | 詩》/the Odes | 三/three | 百，/hundred | ^一/one | 言/phrase, saying | 以/thereby, with it | 蔽/to cover, sum up | 之，/them | ^曰：『/(it) says | 思/(in) thought | 無/nothing, without | 邪。』」=xié/crooked, swerving",
  l="The Master said: “The Odes, three hundred —\none phrase covers them:\nit says, ‘In thought, nothing crooked.’”",
  i="The Master said: “The Odes number three hundred, and a single phrase sums them all up: ‘No crookedness in thought.’”",
  n="The Odes held 305 poems; 'three hundred' is the round number (Zhu Xi: 舉大數). 一言 is 'one phrase', not 'one word' — 言 is a saying, here three characters. 以蔽之 is 以 with its object left out, 'with (it) cover them': Zhu Xi glosses 蔽 as 蓋 'to cover, to sum up'. 思無邪 is a line from the ode 駉 (Odes 297), where it describes horses running straight. In the ode 思 may be only a filler particle; Confucius, and Zhu Xi after him, take it as 'thought', and so the line becomes a verdict on the whole collection: whether a poem shows good or bad, its use is to leave the reader's feelings set straight. 邪 is read xié 'crooked'; the reading yé is a question particle."),
