dict(pas="2.4", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^吾/I | 十/ten | 有=yòu/and (= 又 yòu, joining numbers) | 五/five | 而/(at that age) | 志/set my intent | 于/on | 學，/learning",
  l="The Master said: “I, at ten and five, set my intent on learning;",
  i="The Master said: “At fifteen my intent was fixed on learning;",
  n="This passage is Confucius' account of his own life, one stage to a decade. 十有五 'ten and five' is the old way of writing fifteen: 有 between the tens and the units is read yòu, 'and moreover' (later 又). 而 here simply joins the age to what happened at it. 志 is where the heart is aimed. Zhu Xi notes that in antiquity a boy entered the 'greater learning' at fifteen, and takes 學 to be that learning — the way of the great school. He Yan's text reads 志乎學, the same sense."),
dict(w="^三/three | 十/ten | 而/(at that age) | 立，/stood, was established",
  l="at thirty, established;",
  i="at thirty I was established in it;",
  n="Each stage is the same three-part line: an age, 而, a verb. 立 is 'to stand': Zhu Xi says he could stand by himself, holding firm to what he had learned, with no further need to fix his intent."),
dict(w="^四/four | 十/ten | 而/(at that age) | 不/not | 惑，/perplexed, misled",
  l="at forty, not perplexed;",
  i="at forty I was no longer misled;",
  n="惑 is to be confused, led astray. Zhu Xi: nothing in the world's affairs left him in doubt — he knew clearly, and no longer had to hold on by effort."),
dict(w="^五/five | 十/ten | 而/(at that age) | 知=zhī/knew | 天命，/the decree of Heaven//heaven//decree, command",
  l="at fifty, knew the decree of Heaven;",
  i="at fifty I knew what Heaven had ordained;",
  n="天命 'the decree of Heaven' is the Zhou idea that Heaven 天 confers a charge 命 — on a dynasty, and here on a person. Zhu Xi makes it the working of the heavenly Way in all things, 'the reason things are as they ought to be' (事物所以當然之故): at forty he saw what each thing ought to be; at fifty he knew why."),
dict(w="^六/six | 十/ten | 而/(at that age) | 耳/the ear | 順，/(was) compliant, went with",
  l="at sixty, the ear compliant;",
  i="at sixty what I heard went in without resistance;",
  n="耳順 'the ear compliant' is the most puzzling stage. Zhu Xi: 聲入心通 'sounds entered and the heart understood' — whatever he heard, he grasped at once without having to think it through. Master Cheng puts it shortly: 'all that he heard got through'."),
dict(w="^七/seven | 十/ten | 而/(at that age) | 從=cóng/to follow | 心/the heart, the mind | 所/that which | 欲，/(it) wanted | ^不/not | 踰/to overstep, cross | 矩。」/the square — the carpenter's square, the standard",
  l="at seventy, following what the heart wanted,\nnot overstepping the square.”",
  i="at seventy I could go where my heart wished and never stray beyond the carpenter's square.”",
  n="從 is cóng 'to follow' (Zhu Xi: 從如字, the ordinary reading; 從 also stands for 縱 zòng 'to give rein to', and He Yan's text writes 縱). 心所欲 is 'what the heart wanted': 所 turns 欲 'to want' into 'that which (it) wanted'. 矩 is the carpenter's square, the tool that makes corners true; Zhu Xi glosses it 法度之器. The point is that desire and rule have become one. Master Cheng says the sage may not have needed these stages himself and set them out as a pattern for learners; Zhu Xi adds that it was no mock modesty — the sage did feel his own progress, though others could not see it."),
