dict(pas="2.8", w="子夏/Zixia | 問/asked about | 孝。/filial devotion | ^子/the Master | 曰：「/said | 色/the countenance | 難。=nán/is hard | ^有/there is | 事，/work, a task | 弟子=dì zǐ/the young//younger brother//son | 服/take on | 其/its | 勞；/toil, labour | ^有/there is | 酒/wine | 食，=shí/food — Zhu Xi reads sì 'cooked grain' | 先生/the elders — fathers and elder brothers//first, before//born | 饌：/are fed, served | ^曾=céng/ever (Zhu Xi: = 嘗) | 是/this | 以/(is) taken | 為=wéi/as | 孝/filial devotion | 乎？」/(question)",
  l="Zixia asked about filial devotion.\nThe Master said: “The countenance is hard.\nThere is work — the young take on its toil;\nthere is wine and food — the elders are served:\nis this ever taken as filial devotion?”",
  i="Zixia asked about being a good son. The Master said: “The hard part is the look on your face. When there is work to be done, the young take the labour on themselves; when there is wine and food, it goes to their elders — but can that alone be counted as being a good son?”",
  n="色難 'the countenance is hard': two characters, a whole teaching. Zhu Xi explains that deep love shows itself as warmth, warmth as a glad face, a glad face as gentleness — and that is the hard thing; running errands and serving meals are not enough. An older reading he records: 'what is hard is reading your parents' faces and going along with them'. 弟子 here is 'the young' as in 1.6, and 先生 'those born first', the elders (Zhu Xi: 父兄 'fathers and elder brothers') — not yet 'teacher' or 'Mr'. 食 Zhu Xi reads sì, 'cooked grain, a meal'; modern practice reads shí. 曾 is céng, 'ever' (Zhu Xi: 曾猶嘗也); others read zēng, 'after all, can it be that'. 以為 'to take as': 是以為孝 'is this taken as filial?' The four answers on filial devotion (2.5–2.8), Master Cheng says, each fit the man who asked."),
