dict(pas="2.14", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^君子/the gentleman | 周/(is) inclusive, comprehensive | 而/and | 不/not | 比；=bǐ/partisan, cliquish (Zhu Xi reads bì) | ^小人/the small man — the petty man, the gentleman's opposite//small//person | 比=bǐ/partisan | 而/and | 不/not | 周。」/inclusive",
  l="The Master said: “The gentleman is all-round and not partisan;\nthe small man partisan and not all-round.”",
  i="The Master said: “The gentleman is open to everyone and does not form cliques; the small man forms cliques and is open to no one.”",
  n="小人 'the small man' is the 君子's opposite, and you will meet the pair constantly; Zhu Xi says they differ 'like dark and light, night and day'. Both 周 and 比 mean being close to people. 周 is all-round (Zhu Xi: 普徧 'universal'), 比 is one-sided (偏黨 'partisan'); the difference is between public-minded and private. Zhu Xi reads 比 bì, the old reading for 'to band together'; modern practice reads bǐ. Notice how 而不 joins the clauses: 'A, and not B', then reversed for the small man."),
